Title: "Logic"
Id: "2675f869-5535-45d3-9009-0a76ffa07961"


Text: "Mathematics begins with assertion. A mathematical statement—often called
       a *proposition*—is a declarative sentence that is definitively either
       true or false, but never both, never neither, and never indeterminate.
       This classical ideal, known as the *principle of bivalence*, provides the
       bedrock upon which mathematical reasoning stands. We do not require that
       we immediately *know* whether a given proposition is true or
       false—Goldbach's conjecture and the Riemann hypothesis are propositions
       whose truth values remain actively pursued—only that a definite truth
       value exists independently of our knowledge.

       To build complex mathematical theories from elementary observations, we
       require a systematic grammar for combining and transforming propositions.
       This branch of foundational mathematics is *propositional logic*. In this
       setting, atomic statements are treated as indivisible units, abstracted
       as variables $P, Q, R, \ldots$. By applying a small collection of
       truth-functional connectives, we can assemble compound statements whose
       truth or falsity is completely determined by the truth values of their
       constituent parts.

       The logical connectives formulated in this chapter are entirely
       topic-neutral: they operate according to universal rules that apply
       equally whether the underlying assertions concern prime numbers,
       topological spaces, continuous functions, or abstract algebraic groups.
       They are the universal syntax of mathematical discourse."
Id: "b03f7ce2-7aed-4c81-a6e7-d998ccf65747"


SectionTitle: "Conditionals and Equivalence"
Id: "706c5ccb-8690-478e-b0fd-90e906f0276e"


Text: "Virtually every mathematical theorem is formulated as a conditional
       deduction: under the hypothesis that a premise $P$ holds, we guarantee
       that a conclusion $Q$ necessarily follows. In formal logic, this
       relationship is captured by material implication, which is purely
       truth-functional: the implication fails if and only if the antecedent is
       verified while the consequent is refuted.

       When the antecedent $P$ is false, the implication holds vacuously—an
       essential convention ensuring that universal mathematical statements
       remain valid without requiring artificial provisos for degenerate cases:"
Id: "7529a4ce-e5cc-46c7-85b9-fdcd19351351"


[P \.implies./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. if: P
  then: Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ implies $Q?$"
. written: "P? \Rightarrow Q?"
. description: "Material implication asserting that truth of the antecedent $P$
                is sufficient to guarantee the truth of the consequent $Q$. In
                classical bivalence, a conditional with a false antecedent holds
                vacuously, preventing false premises from generating formal
                counterexamples."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "dc7e213d-76c2-43d7-acf1-c5978daaab33"


Text: "While a one-way conditional establishes sufficiency in one direction and
       necessity in the other, mathematical definitions and exact
       characterizations require mutual entailment. When two assertions imply
       each other, they stand or fall together, possessing identical truth
       values under every valuation. This mutual implication is formalized as
       the biconditional, providing the foundational grammar for definitions and
       classifications across mathematics:"
Id: "b60eca2f-6be8-4e23-a2f2-b56e2378684b"


[P \.iff./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. have: P
  iff: Q
Documented:
. called:
  . "$P?$ if and only if $Q?$"
  . "$P?$ iff $Q?$"
. written: "P? \iff Q?"
. description: "Logical biconditional asserting identical truth values between
                $P$ and $Q$. It provides the formal syntax for mathematical
                definition and exact classification, establishing mutual
                sufficiency and necessity."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "96440ac7-3331-4467-a7ac-593dbca0761b"


SectionTitle: "Boolean Connectives"
Id: "faacb189-b227-404c-8a8a-b6e976fc828a"


Text: "Beyond conditional deduction, mathematical reasoning requires combining
       assertions into compound statements. In his 1854 treatise *The Laws of
       Thought*, George Boole demonstrated that logical propositions satisfy
       algebraic laws analogous to the arithmetic of numbers. The most
       elementary combination demands that two statements hold in concert,
       behaving as a logical meet or product:"
Id: "1d61c6bd-bf4c-4c3e-a6dd-769d5c662a6f"


[P \.and./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. allOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ and $Q?$"
. written: "P? \land Q?"
. description: "Truth-functional conjunction acting as the infimum in the
                Boolean lattice of truth values. Holds strictly when both
                constituent assertions hold simultaneously."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "e024d041-fcf1-476e-b29e-b75d88b04fa8"


Text: "In contrast to conjunction, which requires the simultaneous truth of both
       assertions, mathematical statements frequently guarantee only that at
       least one of several alternatives obtains. In colloquial speech, the word
       'or' often connotes mutual exclusivity, but mathematics decisively adopts
       the inclusive interpretation: an alternative holds provided at least one
       constituent assertion is verified, failing solely when both fail:"
Id: "fd7a79c3-4380-4621-a699-eb6902667669"


[P \.or./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. anyOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ or $Q?$"
. written: "P? \lor Q?"
. description: "Inclusive disjunction acting as the supremum in the Boolean
                lattice of truth values. Holds when at least one constituent
                assertion holds, failing solely when both fail."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "f401f869-7009-411a-bc01-9a76ffa07901"


Text: "Complementing binary combinations that synthesize pairs of statements,
       negation is the fundamental unary operation of logic. Governed by the
       classical principle of bivalence, denying an assertion reverses its truth
       value, leaving no intermediate status:"
Id: "856ad9cc-c17e-4cff-ad32-10ee78ab20c4"


[\not{P}]
States:
when: P is \\statement
that:
. not: P
Documented:
. called: "not $P?$"
. written: "\neg P+?"
. description: "Truth-functional negation acting as the complementation
                involution $\neg: \{0,1\} \to \{0,1\}$. Inverts the truth value
                under classical bivalence."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "c28b5247-41fe-455b-80df-261056c1eb02"


Text: "Although conjunction, disjunction, and negation suffice to generate all
       truth functions, mathematical practice frequently encounters cases where
       alternatives are strictly incompatible—such as an integer being either
       even or odd. This strict dichotomy is formalized as exclusive
       disjunction, which mirrors symmetric difference in set theory and
       addition in the two-element field $\mathbb{F}_2$:"
Id: "009e13c8-8dba-4b31-9958-f8531eb5ec04"


[P \.xor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: (P \.or./ Q) \.and./ \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ xor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \text{ xor } Q?"
. description: "Exclusive disjunction realizing addition in the Galois field
                $\mathbb{F}_2$. Holds precisely when propositions differ in
                truth value, corresponding to the symmetric difference of sets."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "1c7bc638-0db8-416f-a0d0-f5c0d7d92842"


Text: "From the perspective of logical economy, one may ask what minimal set of
       connectives suffices to generate all Boolean truth functions. In
       pioneering work by Charles Sanders Peirce and Henry Sheffer, it was shown
       that a single binary operation can generate the entire propositional
       calculus. The first of these universal operators is alternative denial,
       or NAND, which asserts that two statements cannot both be true:"
Id: "2d5efb63-58bb-436f-9aa6-648a2c05e6b6"


[P \.nand./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nand $Q?$"
. written: "P? \barwedge Q?"
. description: "Alternative denial (Sheffer stroke) asserting that $P$ and $Q$
                are not jointly true. Serves as a single functionally complete
                universal connective for classical propositional logic."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "ac552ed6-48a1-4639-940a-9d9a291c76e4"


Text: "Dual to alternative denial is joint denial, or NOR, which asserts that
       neither of two statements holds. Like NAND, it constitutes a single
       functionally complete connective:"
Id: "80e4835a-9179-499e-8fe0-aeeb0cf83679"


[P \.nor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.or./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \downarrow Q?"
. description: "Joint denial (Peirce arrow) asserting that neither $P$ nor $Q$
                holds. Dual to NAND, it constitutes a single functionally
                complete basis generating all Boolean truth functions."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "a16193ad-6084-494c-afd2-b4f7c6a70a7d"


SectionTitle: "Fundamental Laws of Propositional Logic"
Id: "1b3ef4fc-1add-407c-84c2-ac7234d8e0f9"


Text: "When propositions are considered up to logical equivalence, the
       connectives satisfy algebraic identities that give the collection of
       propositions the structure of a Boolean algebra. The most elementary
       identity governs repeated negation: under classical bivalence, denying a
       denial restores the original assertion, providing the logical foundation
       for indirect proof:"
Id: "ddf66eda-3e4a-4b16-a628-eaa5d5dbc270"


Theorem:
given: P is \\statement
then: \not{\not{P}} \.iff./ P
Documented:
. called: "Law of Double Negation"
. description: "Classical involution law asserting that denying a contradiction
                recovers the positive proposition: $\neg(\neg P) \iff P$.
                Foundational to non-constructive proof by contradiction."
Proof: """Under classical bivalence, the statement {.P.} has two possible truth
          values. If {.P.} is true, its negation {.\not{P}.} is false, and the
          negation of that result {.\not{\not{P}}.} is true. Conversely, if
          {.P.} is false, {.\not{P}.} is true, and {.\not{\not{P}}.} is false.
          In both cases, {.\not{\not{P}}.} has the identical truth value as
          {.P.}. Therefore, {.\not{\not{P}} \.iff./ P.}."""
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "eb21571a-d245-427d-a014-d29407d09f9a"


Text: "The interaction of negation with conjunction and disjunction reveals a
       profound order-reversing duality: negating a conjunction yields a
       disjunction of negations, while negating a disjunction yields a
       conjunction of negations. Formulated by Augustus De Morgan, these
       identities anticipate the complementary behavior of unions and
       intersections in set theory, as well as the duality between universal and
       existential quantifiers:"
Id: "2f552add-aa29-4824-b7d0-40345e29c312"


Theorem:
given: P, Q is \\statement
then:
. \not{P \.and./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q})
. \not{P \.or./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.and./ \not{Q})
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's Laws for Logic"
. description: "Order-reversing duality isomorphisms between lattice meet
                ($\land$) and join ($\lor$) mediated by negation:
                $\neg(P \land Q) \iff \neg P \lor \neg Q$ and
                $\neg(P \lor Q) \iff \neg P \land \neg Q$."
Proof: """We evaluate the statements under all four assignments of truth values
          to {.P.} and {.Q.}:

          1. If both {.P.} and {.Q.} are true, then {.P \.and./ Q.} is true, so
             {.\not{P \.and./ Q}.} is false. Meanwhile, {.\not{P}.} and
             {.\not{Q}.} are both false, so {.\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q}.} is false.
             Both sides agree.

          2. If at least one of {.P.} or {.Q.} is false, then {.P \.and./ Q.} is
             false, so {.\not{P \.and./ Q}.} is true. At the same time, at least
             one of {.\not{P}.} or {.\not{Q}.} is true, so
             {.\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q}.} is true. Again, both sides agree.

          The dual identity
          {.\not{P \.or./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.and./ \not{Q}).} follows by
          applying the first identity to {.\not{P}.} and {.\not{Q}.} together
          with the law of double negation."""
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{17}
Id: "e4c6ef38-dd37-448b-a1f5-377df32138ce"


SectionTitle: "Truth Assignments and Expressive Power"
Id: "1fba02b7-9700-4256-a888-1a44c75c3c02"


Text: "Having defined individual connectives and their algebraic laws, we now
       turn to the overarching semantic framework of propositional logic. We
       wish to understand how the truth value of an arbitrary, arbitrarily
       nested formula is determined by the values of its atomic variables, and
       what totality of functions can be expressed within this syntax."
Id: "3e84617a-51ea-42ce-9065-27a36e676101"


TextDefinition: "A *truth assignment* (or valuation) on a set of propositional
                 variables is a function mapping each variable to one of the
                 values $\{\text{true}, \text{false}\}$. Any truth assignment
                 extends uniquely and recursively to all compound formulas
                 according to the truth tables of their connectives.

                 A formula is called a *tautology* (or logically valid) if it
                 evaluates to true under every possible truth assignment; it is
                 a *contradiction* (or unsatisfiable) if it evaluates to false
                 under every assignment; and it is *contingent* if it is true
                 under some assignments and false under others. Tautologies
                 represent universal logical truths, independent of any factual
                 state of affairs."
Id: "e6a57aaf-f2e1-43ce-9979-261056c1eba7"


Text: "A central question of logical syntax is whether an arbitrary compound
       proposition can be reduced to a canonical, structured form. The following
       theorem guarantees that every formula can be presented in two dual normal
       forms: a disjunction of elementary conjunctions (useful in disjunctive
       case analysis) and a conjunction of elementary disjunctions (essential
       for automated theorem proving and clausal resolution):"
Id: "81e3a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d501"


TextTheorem: "Every propositional formula is logically equivalent to one in
              *Disjunctive Normal Form* (DNF) and to one in *Conjunctive Normal
              Form* (CNF).

              A formula in DNF is a disjunction of conjunctions of literals
              (variables or their negations), analogous to a sum-of-products in
              elementary algebra. It can be constructed directly from the
              formula's truth table by taking a conjunction for each row where
              the formula evaluates to true, and forming the disjunction of
              these conjunctions.

              Dually, a formula in CNF is a conjunction of disjunctions of
              literals (a product-of-sums). Although the transformation into
              either normal form can produce an exponential increase in formula
              length, the normal forms guarantee that every Boolean truth
              function admits a completely explicit, canonical representation."
Id: "a415c422-9e69-431e-b2fe-8a833231b229"


Text: "The constructive procedure for converting any truth table into normal
       form establishes that the classical connectives can express every
       conceivable truth function. From this, we obtain the decisive
       completeness theorem for the Sheffer stroke and Peirce arrow:"
Id: "5b736e47-8a4d-451e-9a18-971c26a57891"


TextTheorem: "The singleton connective sets consisting only of NAND
              ($\{\barwedge\}$) or only of NOR ($\{\downarrow\}$) are
              *functionally complete*: every Boolean truth function of any
              number of variables can be expressed using formulas containing
              that single connective alone.

              Functional completeness guarantees that our logical universe lacks
              nothing in expressive reach. In subsequent chapters, when we speak
              of mathematical definitions, properties, and constraints, we do so
              with the certainty that any finite truth condition can be
              formulated rigorously within the language of propositional logic."
Id: "e5bdaf8f-ebd3-4925-ac4c-5e7cad6e9f64"
